# Modules kept in the RECOVERY slot (p7), and why this list is short.
#
# The stock module tree is 212 MiB. p7 is 450 MiB total, and the rest of a
# usable recovery system does not fit alongside it. Rather than guess at what
# is safe to delete, this list was taken from `lsmod` on THIS hardware running
# THIS kernel -- it is what the board actually loads, not what it might.
# build-rootfs.sh --keep-modules resolves each entry's dependencies through
# modules.dep and deletes everything else, then re-runs depmod.
#
# Two things make this safe that would not be true on a general-purpose image:
#   * the drivers that must work before any module can load -- eMMC, ext4, the
#     Ethernet MAC/PHY -- are built into the kernel (CONFIG_MMC=y, MMC_BLOCK=y,
#     MMC_MESON_GX=y, EXT4_FS=y, REALTEK_PHY=y).
#   * CONFIG_TUN=y, so Tailscale needs no module either. That is what lets the
#     recovery slot keep Tailscale, which is the entire point of it: a slot you
#     can reach from anywhere beats a slot you can reach from the same room.
#
# Anything absent here simply does not load in recovery. Audio and video are in
# the list only because the board loads them; nothing in recovery needs them,
# and dropping them later is a safe further saving.
autofs4
axg_audio
binfmt_misc
cfg80211
clk_phase
dwmac_generic
efi_pstore
ge2d
ip_tables
mc
meson_rng
nf_conntrack
nf_defrag_ipv4
nf_defrag_ipv6
nf_nat
nf_tables
nfnetlink
nft_chain_nat
nft_compat
nvmem_meson_efuse
polyval_ce
reset_meson_audio_arb
rfkill
sch_fq_codel
sclk_div
scpi_hwmon
v4l2_mem2mem
videobuf2_common
videobuf2_dma_contig
videobuf2_memops
videobuf2_v4l2
videodev
x_tables
xt_MASQUERADE
xt_connmark
xt_conntrack
xt_mark
xt_tcpudp
